SwiftUI ArrangementView automatically lays out two related views as the available space changes **.** You provide a primary view and a secondary view, then choose a split or overlay style.[SwiftUI](https://bitrig.com/blog/swift-vs-swiftui) adapts its placement to the layout environment, including the fold on iPhone Duo.


That means you don't have to write separate layout logic for every pose. You decide what deserves space, what can disappear, and how the content should look when its layout changes and then ArrangementView does the rest.


The[ArrangementView API](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/arrangementview) is available on iOS 27.1 and later and this guide covers its split and overlay styles using Apple’s documentation as of November 2026.


##
When should you use SwiftUI ArrangementView?


Use ArrangementView when two pieces of content belong together on the same screen, but their placement needs to adapt.


In our recipe example, the ingredients and instructions are the primary content. A photo of the finished meal is the secondary content. Both belong to the same recipe, but they don’t need equal space in every layout.


The two styles support different relationships:


Style


How it arranges content


When it fits


Split


Gives the views separate areas, horizontally or vertically


Both views should remain unobscured when displayed together


Overlay


Places the primary view over the secondary view on a flat display, then separates them around an active fold


The views have a foreground and background relationship


ArrangementView handles the content’s layout. For a recipe list that opens individual recipe screens, use a navigation container such as` NavigationStack` or` NavigationSplitView` .


An ArrangementView can sit inside a NavigationStack to organize a recipe screen. It doesn’t manage navigation history or replace your app’s navigation structure. Apple demonstrates this setup in its[adaptive layouts talk](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/tech-talks/111463/) .


##
How do you create a split ArrangementView?


Start by providing your two views and applying the split style:


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeDetailView()
} secondary: {
FoodImageView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.split)
```


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeDetailView()
} secondary: {
FoodImageView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.split)
```


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeDetailView()
} secondary: {
FoodImageView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.split)
```


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeDetailView()
} secondary: {
FoodImageView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.split)
```


A split arrangement generally places its views side by side when its area is wider than it is tall, and stacks them vertically when it’s taller than it is wide. Size classes and reserved regions also affect the result.


A **reserved region** is an area the interface needs to accommodate, such as the fold dividing the Duo’s inner display. Apple explains these regions in its[iPhone Duo design guidance](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/designing-for-iphone-duo) .


Avoid assuming “open means horizontal” and “closed means vertical.” The actual environment matters. For example, Apple documents that a[split arrangement](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/splitarrangementviewstyle) shows only its primary view on a closed Duo in landscape.


So that's the default behavior. But we can make some customizations and adjustments to dial in an ArrangementView to be what we need for our UI.


###
How do you change the split ratio?


Do the instructions and photo really need the same amount of space? In this app, I’d rather give the recipe more room.


Apply`[splitArrangementLayoutRatio(_:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/view/splitarrangementlayoutratio(_:))` to the primary view:


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeContentView()
.splitArrangementLayoutRatio(0.65)
} secondary: {
RecipePhotoView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.split)
```


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeContentView()
.splitArrangementLayoutRatio(0.65)
} secondary: {
RecipePhotoView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.split)
```


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeContentView()
.splitArrangementLayoutRatio(0.65)
} secondary: {
RecipePhotoView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.split)
```


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeContentView()
.splitArrangementLayoutRatio(0.65)
} secondary: {
RecipePhotoView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.split)
```


A value of` 0.65` requests about 65% of the available space for the recipe, leaving about 35% for the photo when that preference can be honored.


This is a preferred ratio, not a fixed frame. Available space and layout priorities affect the result, and the arrangement still needs to accommodate the fold. Don’t assume the same percentages will hold in every pose.


###
How do you adapt content to the split direction?


A summary row with cooking time and serving information might look great beside the photo but feel cramped when the views stack vertically when the iPhone Duo is closed and in portrait.


The`[splitArrangementAxis](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/environmentvalues/splitarrangementaxis)` environment value lets a child view read the current split direction (horizontal or vertical):


```text
struct RecipeContentView: View {
@Environment( \.  splitArrangementAxis)
private var splitAxis


var body: some View {
VStack(alignment: .leading) {
if splitAxis == .horizontal {
RecipeSummaryView()
}


RecipeInstructionsView()
}
}
}
```


```text
struct RecipeContentView: View {
@Environment( \.  splitArrangementAxis)
private var splitAxis


var body: some View {
VStack(alignment: .leading) {
if splitAxis == .horizontal {
RecipeSummaryView()
}


RecipeInstructionsView()
}
}
}
```


```text
struct RecipeContentView: View {
@Environment( \.  splitArrangementAxis)
private var splitAxis


var body: some View {
VStack(alignment: .leading) {
if splitAxis == .horizontal {
RecipeSummaryView()
}


RecipeInstructionsView()
}
}
}
```


```text
struct RecipeContentView: View {
@Environment( \.  splitArrangementAxis)
private var splitAxis


var body: some View {
VStack(alignment: .leading) {
if splitAxis == .horizontal {
RecipeSummaryView()
}


RecipeInstructionsView()
}
}
}
```


Here, the extra summary appears only in a horizontal arrangement when there is plenty of vertical space. When the views are split vertically, it is not shown because vertical screen real estate is limited.


The value describes the arrangement’s axis, rather than directly telling you whether the phone is open or closed. Read it inside the content that ArrangementView is arranging.


With this API, you can make adjustments to your UI based on whether the views are split horizontally or vertically.


###
How do you hide optional secondary content?


Even with a smaller photo, a vertically stacked recipe screen might still feel crowded. Personally, I’d rather see more instructions. Restrict the split style to the horizontal axis:


```text
.arrangementViewStyle(.split.axes(.horizontal))
```


```text
.arrangementViewStyle(.split.axes(.horizontal))
```


```text
.arrangementViewStyle(.split.axes(.horizontal))
```


```text
.arrangementViewStyle(.split.axes(.horizontal))
```


When the layout would use an unsupported vertical split (like when the iPhone Duo is closed and in portrait), the arrangement displays only its primary view. The recipe stays visible and the photo disappears. This behavior is documented in Apple’s[split arrangement reference](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/splitarrangementviewstyle) .


That’s a useful option when the secondary view is optional. A decorative food photo can disappear without stopping someone from following the recipe. An essential action or the only copy of the ingredient list needs to remain accessible.


##
How does the overlay style work?


An overlay arrangement fits a screen with a clear foreground and background, such as a recipe card floating over a food photo.


The setup is similar:


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeDetailView()
} secondary: {
FoodImageView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.overlay)
```


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeDetailView()
} secondary: {
FoodImageView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.overlay)
```


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeDetailView()
} secondary: {
FoodImageView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.overlay)
```


```text
ArrangementView {
RecipeDetailView()
} secondary: {
FoodImageView()
}
.arrangementViewStyle(.overlay)
```


Here’s the easy detail to get backward: **the primary view is the foreground view.** The secondary view goes behind it, even if the background takes up most of the screen.


With the default[overlay style](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/overlayarrangementviewstyle) , a flat display shows the primary view over the secondary view. This includes the Duo’s fully open inner display and its closed outer display.


When the inner display is partially folded, the views separate around the fold. In landscape, they sit beside each other. In portrait, one sits above the other.


But is this really what we want? Of course this depends on your UI, but in the example above our primary view is a small card. So that looks weird when it gets an entire side of the fold by itself. Don't worry. We can fix this.


###
How do you expand an overlay when the views separate?


The`[overlayArrangementZIndex](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/environmentvalues/overlayarrangementzindex)` environment value reports a view’s position in the arrangement’s stacking order.


Read it inside the primary view to switch between compact and expanded presentations:


```text
struct RecipeOverlayView: View {
@Environment( \.  overlayArrangementZIndex)
private var zIndex: Int


var body: some View {
if zIndex > 0 {
CompactRecipeCard()
} else {
ExpandedRecipeDetails()
}
}
}
```


```text
struct RecipeOverlayView: View {
@Environment( \.  overlayArrangementZIndex)
private var zIndex: Int


var body: some View {
if zIndex > 0 {
CompactRecipeCard()
} else {
ExpandedRecipeDetails()
}
}
}
```


