private var zIndex: Int


var body: some View {
if zIndex > 0 {
CompactRecipeCard()
} else {
ExpandedRecipeDetails()
}
}
}
```


This follows the pattern in[Apple’s example](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/tech-talks/111463/) : when the primary view’s z-index is greater than zero, it’s overlaid, so show the compact version. When the views separate, use the expanded version.


The z-index belongs to the individual view. It isn’t a general fold detector, and reading it from the background view doesn’t give you the same test.


For our recipe app, the compact version might offer a short summary and access to the recipe. The expanded version can make better use of its region by showing more details. Keep the same functionality available in both presentations.


###
What happens if you restrict overlay axes?


Overlay arrangements also support axis restrictions:


```text
.arrangementViewStyle(.overlay.axes(.vertical))
```


```text
.arrangementViewStyle(.overlay.axes(.vertical))
```


```text
.arrangementViewStyle(.overlay.axes(.vertical))
```


```text
.arrangementViewStyle(.overlay.axes(.vertical))
```


Choose this carefully. Apple’s current[overlay axes documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/overlayarrangementviewstyle/axes(_:)) says that excluded axes display only the primary view.


That means you shouldn’t rely on this modifier to keep both views overlaid when the layout would otherwise separate horizontally.


For this recipe design, I’d keep the default axes and adapt the foreground content with` overlayArrangementZIndex` . That preserves the photo while giving the recipe a presentation that suits its new space.


##
What should you test before shipping?


Once the layout works, walk through the ways someone will actually use it:


-


**Open, partially folded, and closed:** Check both portrait and landscape where your app supports them.


-


**Essential content:** Make sure ingredients, instructions, and important actions remain accessible when secondary content disappears.


-


**The fold:** Check that important text and controls stay visible and comfortable to use.


-


**Compact and expanded overlays:** Confirm both presentations are useful and preserve the same functionality.


-


**Different content sizes:** Try longer recipes and larger text settings, not just the shortest sample.


For broader design principles, our guide to[Apple’s Human Interface Guidelines](https://bitrig.com/blog/apple-human-interface-guidelines) is a useful next step.


##
Frequently asked questions


### Is ArrangementView only for iPhone Duo?


No. ArrangementView also adapts to flat displays, and Apple documents availability across its platforms. On iPhone and iPad, it requires iOS 27.1 or iPadOS 27.1 and later. You can use it for the same related content across supported devices without making the layout exclusive to Duo.


### Do I need to calculate the fold angle myself?


For the standard split and overlay behaviors shown here, no. ArrangementView responds to its layout environment. More specialized layouts may need additional customization, but start with the built-in styles before adding your own fold-related logic.


### Can I put ArrangementView inside a ScrollView?


Apple advises against placing ArrangementView inside scrolling containers such as` ScrollView` or` List` . Let the arrangement organize the screen’s main content regions, then make the content within a region scroll when needed. Apple discusses this distinction in its[adaptive layouts talk](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/tech-talks/111463/) .


##
Make the layout fit the content


If your screen has two related views, SwiftUI ArrangementView gives you a useful starting point. Choose split when the views need separate space, or overlay when they have a foreground and background relationship. Then adjust the content that feels cramped or wastes space.


You don’t need a completely different screen for every pose. You need a few thoughtful decisions about what matters in each layout.


